{"storyRoleAssignments":[{"storyId":"AMSD-1919","agentRole":"checkout-form-engineer"}],"profileAdditions":[{"role":"checkout-form-engineer","rules":["Email field validation in CheckoutForm must normalize both email addresses to lowercase before comparison to avoid case-sensitivity bugs like TRM774 mismatch errors. Normalize input at field blur and again before form submission to ensure downstream handlers receive consistent casing.","When modifying checkout form fields (CheckoutPageComponentMapper.tsx, PurchaseConfirmationPageComponentMapper.tsx, CheckoutPage.tsx), account for Contentstack content mapper structure — forms pull field definitions from ContentStack data, not hardcoded React state. Test with actual ContentStack content shapes, not just mock data.","Jest tests run in jest-environment-jsdom (not node) per jest.config.js — use jsdom-compatible test patterns. Form tests must use @testing-library/react with React Hook Form's useForm patterns, not direct DOM manipulation. setupTests.ts is already configured; add form-specific matchers there if needed.","React Hook Form validation in this codebase uses @hookform/error-message for display and custom validation rules on field definitions. When fixing email validation, update both the field-level validation rule AND the error message i18n keys in the affected component mapper. Never split validation logic across mapper and page component.","Next.js dynamic imports are used throughout CheckoutPage.tsx (Checkout, OrderProcessingContainer, etc.). Dynamic imports execute at render time and reset form state on navigation. When modifying form logic, verify that dynamic imports don't cause unintended form reset or loss of user input on route changes.","Checkout form fields are internationalized via next-i18next (useTranslation hook). Validation error messages must have i18n keys in the component mapper's messages, not hardcoded English strings. Email validation error (TRM774) already has i18n keys; reuse existing keys rather than creating new ones.","CommonJS module system (no ES6 modules). All imports use require() or CommonJS import syntax. No type-only imports that would break at runtime in CommonJS.","When testing email validation logic, test both the comparison at blur time (to show error immediately) and at submit time (to confirm normalized email is submitted). This bug fix requires testing both UX flows."]}],"newProfiles":[{"role":"checkout-form-engineer","profile":"Checkout form specialist for @metrolinx/gotransit (Next.js + TypeScript + React + Jest). Modifies checkout page components (CheckoutPageComponentMapper.tsx, PurchaseConfirmationPageComponentMapper.tsx, CheckoutPage.tsx) that handle email and payment field validation, error display, and form submission. Master React Hook Form patterns used in this codebase (@hookform/error-message imports, useForm, field validation rules). Understand Contentstack CMS content mapping — form fields are defined in ContentStack data objects passed to component mappers, not hardcoded React state. Write tests using jest with jest-environment-jsdom (not Node.js), @testing-library/react, and userEvent for form interactions. Test setup already configured in src/setupTests.ts. Validate test file naming: place tests in __tests__/ directories with .spec.ts or .spec.tsx extensions. All form validation must be internationalized via next-i18next — error messages have i18n keys, never hardcoded strings. Email validation bugs (like case-sensitivity in TRM774 errors) must normalize email case at both field-blur validation and form-submit time to prevent UX errors and ensure data consistency. Account for Next.js dynamic imports in CheckoutPage.tsx; these reset component state on navigation, affecting form field preservation. Never split validation logic across multiple files — all validation for a field stays in its component mapper. Module system is CommonJS; use Node require() and CommonJS syntax, no ES6 modules. Understand that Stripe integration and downstream payment handlers expect normalized email format, so email normalization is part of the validation contract with the backend."}]}
